#{[17592186523674 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{9}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523890 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc58a902-969b-5c49-b676-fba60e52b489" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523996 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523996 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162798" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523996 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523996 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523996 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524047 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524047 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "277fd1b8-f855-56f4-9b8a-ec30a076c788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524047 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524057 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48ac9f84-9cc5-5dd0-b18e-283909b67aa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524064 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524064 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65849aaa-3c3d-5a0b-9d2c-50bf3309805a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524064